WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.823 --> 00:00:03.679
Through the runner and the gate the melt enters the cavity:

2
00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:06.721
here a flat cover with a hole and two ribs.

3
00:00:07.715 --> 00:00:09.584
The melt flows out from the gate.

4
00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:14.962
Its leading edge is the flow front; the last spot is the end of the flow path.

5
00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:19.608
The air escapes through the vents: slots at the parting line,

6
00:00:19.759 --> 00:00:25.970
typically 0.01 to 0.05 millimetres deep, depending on the plastic.

7
00:00:26.678 --> 00:00:27.967
Too thin for plastic.

8
00:00:29.046 --> 00:00:32.587
Then holding pressure packs in more plastic against the shrinkage;

9
00:00:32.761 --> 00:00:34.700
the part cools and is ejected.

10
00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:38.203
A defect can form in each of those steps.

11
00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:43.479
A short shot is not full: the flow front does not reach the end.

12
00:00:44.213 --> 00:00:48.833
Causes: too little plastic, pressure or speed, too cold, or trapped air.

13
00:00:49.857 --> 00:00:53.514
Vents clog: a mould that filled last month short-shots now.

14
00:00:54.266 --> 00:00:56.808
Check the vents first, the pressure later.

15
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:02.701
If the air cannot get out, it is compressed and heats up: the diesel effect.

16
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.202
The plastic scorches into burn marks, right at the end.

17
00:01:08.588 --> 00:01:12.605
Flash is the opposite: a thin fin of plastic at the parting line.

18
00:01:13.294 --> 00:01:18.240
Causes: too much pressure, too little clamp force, plastic too hot, or wear.

19
00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:23.632
A crate starts flashing at one corner after eighty thousand shots.

20
00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:29.844
The setter raises the clamp force from 180 to 220 tonnes: gone.

21
00:01:30.826 --> 00:01:34.680
Two weeks later it is back, worse: the parting line is worn.

22
00:01:35.416 --> 00:01:40.165
Mould out, repair the parting line, and back to 180 tonnes.

23
00:01:41.506 --> 00:01:45.105
A sink mark is a dent above a thick section, such as a rib.

24
00:01:45.821 --> 00:01:50.384
The skin sets first; the hot core keeps shrinking and pulls it inwards.

25
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.759
The cause: too little holding pressure, or a gate that freezes too early.

26
00:01:56.476 --> 00:02:01.515
Inside, that gives voids, seen only on an X-ray or in a sawn-through part.

27
00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.086
Behind the hole two flow fronts meet again in a weld line: a thin line,

28
00:02:07.191 --> 00:02:08.874
weaker and sometimes visible.

29
00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:13.744
Silver streaks from the gate are usually moisture from wet material:

30
00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:15.578
then the cause is in the dryer.

31
00:02:16.906 --> 00:02:19.657
In warpage the part bends after ejection,

32
00:02:19.808 --> 00:02:24.046
because it shrinks unevenly: through uneven walls, a hotter mould half,

33
00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.809
aligned fibres, or ejecting too hot.

34
00:02:27.923 --> 00:02:32.439
Often only hours later: good at the machine, rejected at the customer.

35
00:02:33.131 --> 00:02:37.277
So measure after a fixed wait, for example 24 hours.

36
00:02:38.332 --> 00:02:42.523
Before you touch the settings, ask first: which cavity, where on the part,

37
00:02:42.697 --> 00:02:43.463
and since when?

38
00:02:44.791 --> 00:02:46.173
Look before you touch.

39
00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:51.525
One cavity points at the mould, all cavities at the process or the material,

40
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:53.696
now and then at something unstable.

41
00:02:54.706 --> 00:02:59.501
One shot in 20 is short, always in cavity 2: 5 percent of shots,

42
00:02:59.687 --> 00:03:03.565
divided by 4 cavities 1.25 percent of parts.

43
00:03:04.593 --> 00:03:11.420
In a fill study you make short shots on purpose, to 50, 70, 90 and 95 percent full.

44
00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:14.408
They show where the flow front hesitates.

45
00:03:15.401 --> 00:03:18.036
In jetting the plastic squirts from a small gate

46
00:03:18.106 --> 00:03:21.148
into the empty cavity and folds over itself.

47
00:03:21.864 --> 00:03:24.233
Start slower, or aim the gate at a wall.

48
00:03:25.524 --> 00:03:30.400
Change one thing at a time and wait: about ten shots after pressure or speed,

49
00:03:30.574 --> 00:03:33.639
often 20 to 30 minutes after a temperature.

50
00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.170
If it did not help, undo it.

51
00:03:37.235 --> 00:03:40.068
A boundary sample is a part right on the limit,

52
00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.854
signed by quality and kept at the machine.

53
00:03:43.551 --> 00:03:45.931
So every shift judges the same way.

54
00:03:47.208 --> 00:03:49.739
Warpage is always differential shrinkage.

55
00:03:50.451 --> 00:03:52.959
Thick regions and the hotter side shrink more;

56
00:03:53.087 --> 00:03:56.059
about 10 degrees between the halves can be enough.

57
00:03:56.781 --> 00:03:59.823
Glass fibres hold back shrinkage along the flow.

58
00:04:00.792 --> 00:04:06.574
The mould shrinkage: about 0.3 percent along, 0.7 percent across.

59
00:04:07.257 --> 00:04:13.549
Steel at 0.5 percent, 201.0 millimetres, gives 200.4: too long.

60
00:04:14.257 --> 00:04:16.138
Ask for shrinkage per direction.

61
00:04:17.143 --> 00:04:21.717
A box shows the corner effect: the inside corner of the core stays hot,

62
00:04:21.892 --> 00:04:24.411
shrinks more and pulls the walls inwards.

63
00:04:25.457 --> 00:04:29.439
Measure only after conditioning: a fixed time in a fixed climate,

64
00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:35.394
for example 23 degrees and 50 percent humidity; polyamide takes up water.

65
00:04:36.163 --> 00:04:37.916
Then correct the steel once.

66
00:04:39.227 --> 00:04:42.826
Solving a defect for good takes three things: a proven cause,

67
00:04:42.989 --> 00:04:45.276
a known process window and monitoring.

68
00:04:45.976 --> 00:04:49.157
Counting with a Pareto chart and finding causes are

69
00:04:49.238 --> 00:04:52.187
taught in the quality and problem-solving modules.

70
00:04:53.213 --> 00:04:58.646
A DOE, a design of experiments, changes settings together in a planned pattern.

71
00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:05.853
Holding pressure and time, low and high: sinks of 40, 30, 24 and 10 micrometres.

72
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:12.015
The main effect: high pressure makes the sink 12 micrometres shallower on average,

73
00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:13.385
long time 18.

74
00:05:14.071 --> 00:05:19.017
Pressure helps more at the long time, 14 against 10: a small interaction.

75
00:05:20.027 --> 00:05:23.789
A cavity pressure sensor measures the pressure in the cavity every shot.

76
00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.786
Near the end of fill it sees a short shot at once.

77
00:05:28.797 --> 00:05:33.046
With process monitoring a shot outside the limits goes to the reject bin.

78
00:05:33.737 --> 00:05:37.464
Set the limits on a good run: too tight gives false alarms,

79
00:05:37.615 --> 00:05:39.635
too wide lets bad parts through.
